@startuml
title Buffer lifecycle — states partitioned by fill level
skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam StateBackgroundColor #DDF0DD
skinparam StateBorderColor #37474F
[*] --> Empty : alloc(n, unit)\n<latex>C = \mathrm{cap}(n,\mathit{unit}) \le L</latex>
Empty --> Partial : write accepted\n(<latex>0 < \ell < C</latex>)
Empty --> Full : write fills exactly\n(<latex>\ell = C</latex>)
Empty --> Empty : read(n) => 0 bytes\nwriteBytes(empty)
Partial --> Partial : write accepted\nread(n) advances <latex>\rho</latex>
Partial --> Full : write reaches <latex>\ell = C</latex>
Full --> Full : writeBytes => [true, 0]\nwriteByte => [false, BUFERR_CAPACITY_EXCEEDED]\nread(n) advances <latex>\rho</latex>
Empty --> Empty : clear()
Partial --> Empty : clear()
Full --> Empty : clear()
Empty --> Closed : close()
Partial --> Closed : close()
Full --> Closed : close()
Closed --> Closed : any NAMED method => [false, BUFERR_REVOKED]\n(unknown method => BUFERR_UNSUPPORTED)
Closed --> [*]
legend bottom
  Empty: <latex>\ell = 0</latex>    Partial: <latex>0 < \ell < C</latex>    Full: <latex>\ell = C</latex>
  Closed: metadata token is the REVOKED tombstone
  read(n) moves <latex>\rho</latex> only, so it never changes state
endlegend
@enduml
